      {
        "id": "p43-p3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$x=te^{-t}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The particle changes from rightward to leftward at $t=1$, where $x=\\frac{1}{e}$. As $t\\to\\infty$, $x\\to0$ and $v\\to0$. It eventually slows down.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}v(t)&=e^{-t}-te^{-t}\\\\v(t)&=(1-t)e^{-t}\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $e^{-t}>0$, $v>0$ for $0\\le t<1$ and $v<0$ for $t>1$. The particle reverses at $t=1$, where $x(1)=e^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}$.\n\nThe exponential decay dominates the linear factors, so $te^{-t}\\to0$ and $(1-t)e^{-t}\\to0$.\n\n$$a(t)=(t-2)e^{-t}$$\n\nFor $t>2$, $v<0$ and $a>0$, so the particle slows down.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      },
      {
        "id": "p43-p3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$x=\\frac{2t}{1+t^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The particle changes from rightward to leftward at $t=1$, where $x=1$. As $t\\to\\infty$, $x\\to0$ and $v\\to0$. It eventually slows down.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}v(t)&=\\frac{2(1+t^2)-(2t)(2t)}{(1+t^2)^2}\\\\v(t)&=\\frac{2(1-t^2)}{(1+t^2)^2}\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $0\\le t<1$, $v>0$, and for $t>1$, $v<0$. The particle reverses at $t=1$, where $x(1)=\\frac{2}{2}=1$.\n\nThe denominators have higher degree than the numerators, so $x\\to0$ and $v\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a(t)&=\\frac{-4t}{(1+t^2)^2}-\\frac{8t(1-t^2)}{(1+t^2)^3}\\\\a(t)&=\\frac{4t(t^2-3)}{(1+t^2)^3}\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $t>\\sqrt{3}$, $v<0$ and $a>0$, so the particle slows down.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p43-p3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$x=\\frac{1-t^2}{1+t^2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The particle starts at rest at $x=1$ and moves left without reversing. As $t\\to\\infty$, $x\\to-1$ and $v\\to0$. It eventually slows down.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}v(t)&=\\frac{-2t(1+t^2)-(1-t^2)(2t)}{(1+t^2)^2}\\\\v(t)&=\\frac{-4t}{(1+t^2)^2}\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $t=0$, $x=1$ and $v=0$. For all $t>0$, $v<0$, so the particle moves left throughout.\n\nThe leading terms give $x\\to-1$. The denominator of $v(t)$ has higher degree than the numerator, so $v\\to0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a(t)&=\\frac{-4}{(1+t^2)^2}+\\frac{16t^2}{(1+t^2)^3}\\\\a(t)&=\\frac{4(3t^2-1)}{(1+t^2)^3}\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $t>\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$, $v<0$ and $a>0$, so the particle slows down.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p43-p3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$x=t^2e^{-t}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The particle starts at rest and changes from rightward to leftward at $t=2$, where $x=\\frac{4}{e^2}$. As $t\\to\\infty$, $x\\to0$ and $v\\to0$. It eventually slows down.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}v(t)&=2te^{-t}-t^2e^{-t}\\\\v(t)&=t(2-t)e^{-t}\\end{align*}$$\n\nHere $v(0)=0$, $v>0$ for $0<t<2$ and $v<0$ for $t>2$. The reversal occurs at $t=2$, where $x(2)=4e^{-2}=\\frac{4}{e^2}$.\n\nThe exponential decay dominates the polynomial factors, so $x\\to0$ and $v\\to0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}a(t)&=(2-2t)e^{-t}-t(2-t)e^{-t}\\\\a(t)&=(t^2-4t+2)e^{-t}\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $t>2+\\sqrt{2}$, $v<0$ and $a>0$, so the particle slows down.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
